# What each of Catalan's own characters arrives as: the code point a caller
# writes, and the byte the module reads it as. The byte is the Windows Western
# one, because that is what utf8ToWestern answers with; it is not the
# alphabet's own code. The conversion runs on text that is UTF-8, which is
# what a caller should be sending.
#
# a with a grave is the same vowel as a with an acute, and Catalan never
# writes the acute one, so it arrives as that and takes the stress rule
# Castilian already has for it. i and u with a grave are not Catalan at all --
# it writes those two only with an acute -- and are mapped for the same reason,
# so that a stray one is stressed rather than swallowed.
#
# e and o with a grave are not here, and that is the point: they are the open
# vowels, they reach a rule of their own through the arm of the letter chain
# Castilian keeps for n with a tilde, and mapping them onto the acute would
# throw away the one thing in the writing that says which of the two vowels is
# meant. glob.up says how that arm was freed.
00e0 e1   # a with grave, as a with acute
00ec ed   # i with grave, as i with acute
00f9 fa   # u with grave, as u with acute
00c0 c1   # A with grave, as A with acute
00cc cd   # I with grave
00d9 da   # U with grave
00c8 e8   # E with grave, as its own lower case
00d2 f2   # O with grave, as its own lower case
